##* {{quote-journal|en|date=4 Feb 84|volume=11|number=28|journal=Gay Community News|page=5|author=Vincent F. Luti|title=Mouths Open, Only Singing|text=Male colleges '''going''' coed}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|author=Saverio Giovacchini|title=Hollywood Modernism: Film and Politics|isbn=1566398630|page=18
|passage=Referring to the American radicals who '''went''' Hollywood in the 1930s, Abraham Polonsky argues that "you can't possibly explain the Hollywood communists away {{...}}"}}
## To move to (a position or state).
##: {{ux|en|If we can win on Saturday, we'll '''go''' top of the league.}}
##: {{ux|en|They '''went''' level with their rivals.}}
## To come (to a certain condition or state).
##: {{ux|en|They '''went''' into debt.}}
##: {{ux|en|She '''goes''' to sleep around 10 o'clock.}}
# To [[assume]] the [[obligation]] or [[function]] of; to be, to serve as.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1912|title=The Bookseller, Newsdealer and Stationer|volume=36|page=17
|passage=There is scarcely a business man who is not occasionally asked to '''go''' bail for somebody.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=Jane Sanders|title=Youth Justice: Your Guide to Cops and Courts|isbn=1862878129|passage=Most welfare workers are not allowed to '''go''' surety for clients.}}
